a week after prime minister boris  johnson said humanity is reaching a  turning point on climate change there's  been a warning that people's anxiety  levels over the issue is reaching an  all-time high could scaring people too  much end up preventing them from taking  action  how do we find that balance how should  we deal with climate anxiety alice  well the way we talk about climate is  something i'm very preoccupied with how  do we tell this story and how do we make  people feel um  powerful and and not powerless i'm not  sure i have the answer but should we be  concerned about eco anxiety 100  um and i would say that feeling anxiety  in the face of headlines like you know  the ipcc's red code for humanity  scientists telling us untold suffering  is coming down the line if we don't take  action these are entirely rational  responses to the information um that we  are learning i'm hearing about the rise  of climate cafes across the uk sort of  workshop therapy sessions for the young  people and and i i can't help but think  about you know not so long ago young  people when they thought about the  future um it was a future of flying cars  and and now it's  one that is very dystopian but we have  to talk not only about the sacrifice and  not only about the dystopia but also  about the opportunities that we have to  build a better and fairer and greener  world  as you mentioned there alice young  people certainly are the demographic  that are going to have to deal with  climate change for longer and the  demographic that are more concerned  about it 56  of young people according to a recent  poll think humanity is doomed david how  would you reassure them  i think the opportunities are there to  make sure the future is much more  comfortable and much more attractive  than the one they imagine reading the  climate science headlines that's because  dramatic change is quite possible it's  not because the climate science is  um  you know being presented in a too scary  manner that is what the science says we  don't change course we don't take action  the world will not resemble the one that  our parents and grandparents and great  grandparents lived in um the  what we would have to do to live  comfortably in that world is far beyond  our imaginations and we have to take  seriously just how urgent the crisis is  if we hope to respond to it for those  who worry about climate anxiety i think  it is real for people who are  preoccupied with the subject but i also  think it is a motivator i think if we  look at the last few years um after the  ipc's 1.5 degree report we saw the rise  of brother tinberg and the  school strikers we saw the rise of  extinction rebellion in the us we saw  the rise of sunrise and all of global  climate geopolitics has changed in the  aftermath of those protest movements  which were motivated by fear they also  offered positive visions of the future i  think those are important too but they  were driven primarily by anxiety i think  we should respond to how scary the  science is by mobilizing not by um  collapsing into despair but i think we  have to take seriously just how urgent  the science says the matter really is  and alice where would you strike the  balance just briefly before we let you  go uh the chair of the climate change  committee said if you scare the if you  scare the public you don't get people to  act do you believe that's true  uh i think that  as david says it's it's a motivating  factor for me action is the antidote to  anxiety so you know many of us may act  from fear um it is  not so productive to act from a place of  fear so how can you transform that  emotion into something productive  be an activist whatever that means to  you it doesn't necessarily mean standing  out on the street in protest um it means  different things to all of us but find  what you're good at find what you can  contribute to the movement and play your  part  you
 

